WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:03.860
Minimum gas is the gas you must always keep in your tank.

00:04.672 --> 00:09.372
Imagine that, at the deepest point, your buddy signals that they are out of gas.

00:09.412 --> 00:11.912
You share your gas, and you both ascend.

00:12.800 --> 00:15.230
How much gas must you keep in your tank for this?

00:16.330 --> 00:23.970
Minimum gas is the gas two divers need to ascend from the deepest point to the surface, on one tank, under stress.

00:24.798 --> 00:26.858
Courses use different values.

00:27.805 --> 00:32.965
We calculate it with a cat: C times A times T, for two divers.

00:33.185 --> 00:36.145
C is the consumption of both divers.

00:36.225 --> 00:39.285
A is the average pressure during the ascent.

00:39.285 --> 00:41.495
T is the time to ascend.

00:44.095 --> 00:52.855
For example, in our dive the deepest point is twenty metres, and two stressed divers breathe twenty litres per minute each from one eleven point one litre tank.

00:52.855 --> 00:57.485
They take one minute to solve the problem, then ascend at three metres per minute on average.

00:59.235 --> 01:03.155
Step one: let's calculate C, the consumption of both divers.

01:03.155 --> 01:07.075
Twenty litres per minute each, times two divers, is forty litres per minute.

01:08.315 --> 01:12.975
Step two: let's calculate A, the average pressure during the ascent.

01:12.975 --> 01:20.465
Take three atmospheres at twenty metres and one atmosphere at the surface, add them, then divide by two: two atmospheres.

01:22.565 --> 01:26.365
Step three: let's calculate T, the time to ascend.

01:26.445 --> 01:34.195
Twenty metres divided by three metres per minute, plus one minute to solve the problem, is seven point seven minutes, rounded up to eight minutes.

01:35.445 --> 01:40.365
Step four: let's calculate the gas for both divers, C times A times T.

01:40.625 --> 01:45.775
Forty litres per minute times two atmospheres times eight minutes is six hundred and forty litres.

01:48.075 --> 01:51.075
Step five: let's calculate the same gas in bar.

01:51.075 --> 02:00.035
Six hundred and forty litres divided by the tank's volume, eleven point one litres, is fifty-seven point seven bar, rounded up to sixty bar.

02:00.810 --> 02:02.920
That is minimum gas for this dive.

02:06.020 --> 02:09.000
Your plan must never use minimum gas.

02:09.710 --> 02:15.300
When either gauge reaches it, the dive is over and you both ascend, even if the plan says otherwise.

02:16.750 --> 02:26.450
For a single aluminium eighty, minimum gas is forty bar for a deepest point of ten metres, sixty bar for twenty metres, and one hundred bar for thirty metres.

02:26.450 --> 02:29.030
It is never less than forty bar.

02:29.875 --> 02:32.395
Between two depths, use the deeper one.

02:33.138 --> 02:35.708
Deeper than thirty metres, calculate it.

02:38.808 --> 02:43.528
Turn Pressure calculates minimum gas for your dive, and the turn pressure.

02:43.748 --> 02:45.608
Free on the App Store.

02:46.423 --> 02:47.833
diver dot today.
